 The Daily Loaf Bakery Website

 1. Project Overview

The Daily Loaf Bakery is a bakery website developed to provide customers with information about the bakery, its products and services.

The website uses a warm and welcoming design with light beige, warm cream, cream-white and soft golden-brown colours. It is designed to be simple, professional and easy to navigate.

The website was developed using HTML5, CSS3 and JavaScript to create a functional and interactive online presence for the bakery.

 2. Purpose of the Website

The purpose of The Daily Loaf Bakery website is to:

- Introduce the bakery to potential customers.
- Display the bakery's products and menu with prices.
- Provide information about the bakery and its services.
- Allow customers to make enquiries.
- Provide contact details and opening hours.
- Display the bakery's location using Google Maps.
- Allow customers to add products to a shopping cart.
- Provide a checkout process for customer orders.
- Improve the bakery's online presence.

 3. Website Pages

The website consists of the following pages:

 Home Page – index.html

The Home page introduces The Daily Loaf Bakery. It contains a welcome section, bakery images, navigation links and information about the business.

 About Page – about.html

The About page provides information about the bakery, including its background, values and services.

 Menu Page – menu.html

The Menu page displays the bakery's products and prices. Customers can search for products, browse product categories and add selected products to the shopping cart.

 Enquiry Page – enquiry.html

The Enquiry page contains a form that allows customers to send enquiries about bakery products, custom cakes and special orders.

 Contact Page – contact.html

The Contact page provides the bakery's contact information, opening hours and location. An embedded Google Map helps visitors identify the bakery's location.

 Checkout Page – checkout.html

The Checkout page allows customers to review their selected products, view the order total and provide their contact and collection details.

 4. Technologies Used

The website was developed using the following technologies:

- HTML5: Used to create the structure and content of the website.
- CSS3:Used to style the website, including colours, layouts, navigation, buttons and images.
- JavaScript: Used to add interactive features, shopping cart functionality, search and form validation.
- Google Maps: Used to display the bakery's location on the Contact page.
- Web Browser: Used to view and test the website.

 5. Website Design and Layout

The website uses a bakery-inspired colour scheme consisting of:

- Light Beige
- Warm Cream
- Cream White
- Soft Golden Brown
- Brown

These colours create a warm and welcoming appearance that suits a bakery business.

The same colour scheme and styling are used throughout the website to maintain consistency. Navigation links allow visitors to move between the different pages.

The website also uses images to make the pages more attractive and to showcase bakery products.

 6. JavaScript Functionality

JavaScript was added to improve the functionality and interaction of the website.

The JavaScript features include:

 6.1 Shopping Cart

The shopping cart allows customers to:

- Add bakery products to the cart.
- Increase product quantities.
- Remove products from the cart.
- Clear all products from the cart.
- View the total price of selected products.

The shopping cart uses browser local storage to retain cart information when customers move between the menu and checkout pages.

 6.2 Product Search and Categories

The Menu page includes a search function that allows customers to search for bakery products.

Customers can also filter products according to categories, making it easier to find specific items.

6.3 Checkout Functionality

The checkout page displays the selected products and their total cost. Customers can enter their details and provide collection information.

The checkout process uses JavaScript to display the order information and prepare an email order request.

Note: The checkout process prepares an email request. It does not process online payments.*

 6.4 Form Validation

JavaScript is used to check customer form information before submission.

Form validation helps ensure that required information is entered correctly. It also helps display messages when information is missing or invalid.

 7. HTML Forms

HTML forms are used to collect information from customers.

The website includes:

- Enquiry Form: Allows customers to ask questions about products and special orders.
- Contact Form: Allows visitors to send messages to the bakery.
- Checkout Form: Collects customer details and order collection information.

The forms contain suitable input fields, labels and buttons to make them easy to complete.

JavaScript validation is used to improve the accuracy of information entered into the forms.

 8. Search Engine Optimisation (SEO)

Search Engine Optimisation (SEO) helps improve the visibility of a website in search engine results.

The following SEO practices are included or should be checked across the website:

- Each page should have a relevant and descriptive page title.
- Meta descriptions should summarise the content of each page.
- Heading elements should be organised correctly.
- Images should contain descriptive alternative text (alt text).
- HTML elements should be used appropriately.
- The website should have clear navigation links.
- The robots.txt file provides instructions for search engine crawlers.
- The sitemap.xml file lists the main website pages for search engine discovery.

The website uses the name The Daily Loaf Bakery consistently to help identify the business.

 9. Images

Images are used throughout the website to make the pages more visually appealing and to display bakery products.

The main bakery logo is stored in the images folder:

`images/Bakery Logo.jpg.jpg`

The main bakery image is stored in:

`images/bakery.jpg`

Other product images are stored in the same images folder.

Alternative text is used for images to describe their content and improve accessibility.

10. Navigation

The main navigation menu contains five main website pages:

- Home
- About
- Menu
- Enquiry
- Contact

Each page is connected using HTML hyperlinks.

The navigation structure allows visitors to move easily between the pages and access the information they need.

 11. Contact Map

The Contact page contains an embedded Google Map to help visitors identify the bakery's location.

The map is displayed using an HTML iframe and Google Maps Embed functionality.

12. Website Testing

The website should be tested in a web browser to check that its pages and features work correctly.

The following areas should be tested:

- All navigation links open the correct pages.
- Images display correctly.
- The website uses consistent colours and styling.
- The product search returns relevant results.
- Product category buttons filter the menu correctly.
- Products can be added to the shopping cart.
- Cart quantities and totals update correctly.
- Products can be removed from the cart.
- Cart information remains available when moving to checkout.
- Forms identify missing or invalid information.
- The checkout page displays the correct order information.
- The Contact page displays the embedded map.
- The website displays correctly in the selected web browser.

 13. Website Deployment

The website can be published using a free hosting platform such as Netlify or GitHub Pages.

The website files should be uploaded with the correct folder structure so that all HTML pages, CSS, JavaScript and images work correctly.

After deployment, the published website link should be tested to confirm that all pages, images and interactive features work correctly.

